From 33b84707e92ea3a8f5c6704b334de964cca98372 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 10:47:14 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 190/193] probesock: the window of a probe held after the
 connect, then let go

A receive buffer set before the connect has the kernel choose the window
scale by it (tcp_connect_init clamps the window to a buffer the user set):
the window of the connection could never grow past 64 KB, and a
multiplexed transport (gRPC, HTTP/2, xhttp, mux) that keeps the connection
a probe dialed in its pool carried other traffic over it at that window
(pk8, a gRPC server 25 ms away: 7 Mbit/s before and after the buffer was
raised). The window of a probe is now held once the connection is
established, before data moves: a buffer and a window clamp of 20 KB (the
clamp alone is moved by the kernel's tuning of the buffer). After the probe
both go to the system maximum. Over a loopback with 24 ms: 7.5 Mbit/s with
the window of the probe, 554 Mbit/s once it is let go (window_linux_test,
run with a netem delay).
